# Messages from the General Secretary

Messages delivered by General Secretary Annadurai during his address at the Tiruchirappalli District DMK Conference held in Lalgudi:

"Our principles must take up residence in the hearts of all the people of the country. You must spread through every village and hamlet the matters you have heard during the past two days at this conference.

"You comrades of the organisation have conducted yourselves with discipline. Rain is falling, yet you remain where you are without losing heart or moving away. What is the reason for this firmness of mind other than your attachment to the organisation and steadfastness in its principles? If the people of the country acquire this resolve, our work will become easy. That is our most important task today. We must gather the scattered forces together. All of us must focus our attention upon this. Our organisation must stand as a fortress filled with discipline. Once it acquires strength and power, anything can be achieved!

"We see the growth of our organisation with our own eyes. The pavilion is insufficient because such a vast crowd has gathered. What a large pavilion this is! When the conference organisers told me about it on the day before the conference, I asked, 'Such a large pavilion?' Now I see the people gathered even beyond its capacity. Who would not rejoice to see such enthusiasm and affection for our organisation? Our principles and the service that develops and protects them have created this condition.

"This must grow. Once it has grown, we can achieve what we intend and finish what we undertake.

"I am not unaware of our strength. Our strength must be gathered without being scattered. The people's respect for our words has grown. To become fully worthy of that respect, we must possess greater discipline and responsibility towards duty.

"We held a one-day campaign for handloom cloth, and handloom goods lying unsold in stocks worth lakhs were sold. Government statistics state that large quantities of handloom cloth have continued to sell after we observed Handloom Weavers' Support Day. Are we not the reason such enthusiasm has grown among the people? We must devote our attention to similar worthy efforts to remedy the country's distress.

"A resolution has been adopted to change the name Dalmiyapuram. That name stands as a symbol of Tamil Nadu's humiliation. We will certainly conduct a struggle to change it. I am confident that the people of Tamil Nadu will support this struggle. I expect other political parties and public institutions that desire this name to be changed to adopt resolutions and extend their support before the struggle begins.

"Individuals, according to the dictates of their conscience, should write to Pandit Nehru expressing their view that the name must be changed, by telegram if possible or otherwise by postcard. People in each locality may also gather signatures and send them. I ask every progressive party to come forward to change this name, which seems to mock Tamil Nadu."

(*Dravida Nadu*, 3-2-08)
